ures <ei24 include> $ 15 million in costs related to a blast furnace <ei25 outage> and $ 8 million in losses from unauthorized work <ei26 outages> at the company 's coal operations . In the year-ago period , Bethlehem <ei28 earned> $ 101.4 million , or $ 1.27 a share , including a $ 3.8 million <ei29 gain> from early retirement of debt . Third-quarter sales <ei31 dropped> 11 % to $ 1.27 billion from $ 1.43 billion a year ago . In composite trading on the New York Stock Exchange , Bethlehem shares <ei33 rose> 50 cents to $ 17.375 . Of all the major steelmakers , Bethlehem would <ei34 seem> to be the most <ei35 vulnerable> to a <ei36 slowdown> . It has n't <ei37 diversified> beyond steel , nor has it <ei38 linked> up with a joint venture partner to <ei39 share> costs and risks . However , in spite of the difficult industrywide environment of high cost and low volume , Bethlehem \" <ei40 had> pretty good earnings numbers , \" <ei41 said> Michelle Galanter Applebaum , an analyst with Salomon Brothers Inc. Ms. Applebaum had <ei42 estimated> third-quarter earnings of 55 cents a shhha55 cents a share , ents a share , but ssa share , but <ei44 said> the losses for the unusual items were <ei45 larger> than <ei46 expected> . Still , Bethlehem 's core basic steel operations <ei47 experienced> a steep <ei48 drop> in operating profit to $ 58.6 million from $ 186.4 million a year ago , when the industry <ei50 enjoyed> strong demand and pricing . The company <ei51 said> its shipments <ei52 declined> as a result of a <ei54 reduction> in inventories by service centers , a lackluster automotive market and <ei55 increasing> competitive pressures in the construction market . At the same time , production costs , compared with a year ago , were <ei57 boosted> by higher raw material and employment costs , which <ei58 resulted> from the company 's new labor <ei59 pact> effective June 1 . \" We <ei61 anticipate> that steel market conditions will <ei62 exhibit> a further moderate <ei63 decline> in the fourth quarter as the automotive sector <ei66 remains> weak and customers <ei67 continue> to <ei68 adjust> inventories , \" <ei69 said> Bethlehem Chairman Walter F. Williams . He <ei70 noted> , however , that the company 's order entry has <ei71 increased> from the low levels of the early summer , <ei73 following> the end of labor <ei74 negotiations> . Steel business , <ei75 said> third-quarter net income <ei77 dropped> 8 % to $ 33 million , or 35 cents a share , from $ 36 million , or 39 cents a share in the year-ago quarter . Sales <ei79 dropped> to $ 441.1 million from $ 820.4 million , because the company no longer <ei81 consolidates> its Eastern Steel division , which is now a joint <ei83 venture> with Kawasaki Steel Corp. Along with reduced volume , analysts <ei84 said> the nation 's fifth largest steelmaker was <ei85 hurt> by <ei87 holding> higher-cost inventory when raw material costs of such key products as nickel <ei88 dropped> . Operating profit <ei89 dropped> 46 % in its specialty flat-rolled steel segment . Moreover , the company <ei90 said> higher sales and shipments to service centers from its Armco Steel Co. joint venture <ei91 failed> to <ei92 offset> weakness in the automotive market , higher production costs and a poorer product mix . Armco shares <ei93 closed> unchanged at $ 10.625 in composite trading on the New York Stock Exchange . National Intergroup , which <ei94 owns> 50 % of the nation 's sixth largest steelmaker -- National Steel Corp. -- <ei95 posted> net income for the fiscal second-quarter of $ 8.6 million , or 33 cents a share , compared with a net loss of $ 50.3 million . Sales <ei97 increased> in the quarter <ei99 ended> Sept . 30 to $ 747.8 million from $ 623.5 million a year ago . The latest period <ei103 includes> gains of $ 9.1 million from early retirement of debt and tax loss carry-forward . Last year 's results were <ei105 hurt> by $ 41.3 million in restructuring charges . National Intergroup stock <ei106 closed> 